Refining the Settings
Refine the Settings
Run a Simple Simulation Study describes the most basic way of running experiments with the
ExperimentManager. Here you learn how to refine the settings when you Set Static Parameters and
when you Set Dynamic Parameters.
Setting Static Parameters
Set Static Parameters
Static parameters are specific values for each experiment for each parameter. If you want to, you can also
Modify Settings in the Configuration Method.
Modify Settings in the Configuration Method
Under Tools > Advanced Settings > Settings you can configure the settings of your simulation model with
a SimTalk method.
Refining the Settings
10-876 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Click the 
  button, select a Method in the dialog Select Object and click Apply. Plant Simulation asks
if you would like to format the Method. The parameter of data type integer designates the number of
the experiment whose additional settings you can program. You can use all commands which SimTalk
provides. The source code you enter into this Method modifies the settings for the experiment.
Back to Set Static Parameters
Setting Dynamic Parameters
Set Dynamic Parameters
Under Tools > Advanced Settings > Rules you can define dynamic parameters. For this type of
parameterization the results of previous experiments produce the values for the input values of the current
experiment.
A rule is a logic expression that consists of a condition and an action. As soon as the condition of a rule is
met, the associated action of the rule is taken.
First enter the number of experiments you want the ExperimentManager to execute.
Setting Dynamic Parameters
Plant Simulation Help 10-877
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The ExperimentManager checks if the conditions you entered as the table condition and as the method
condition on the tab do apply. It then takes the action(s) which you typed into the table action and
programmed in the method action.
Procedure
1. To use dynamically changing parameters, select the check box use rules.
2. Select one of the built-in rules from the drop-down list Select rule, Create a Rule of Your Own.
3. Select the check box Rule is active, to activate the rule you selected. This rule will then be executed
before the simulation experiment.
4. If you want to, you can enter the number of the experiment(s) for which the rule applies.
5. Enter a Priority. The greater the number you enter, the higher the priority will be: A rule with the
priority 10 is executed before a rule with the priority 1.
6. To use the rule which you selected for the first experiment, select the check box Use for first
simulation run. When you do not select it, the ExperimentManager only uses the rule from the
second experiment onward.
Back to Refine the Settings
Create a Rule of Your Own
In this step we create a rule of our own.
Create a Rule of Your Own
10-878 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Proceed as follows:
Procedure
1. Click the button Create New Rule and enter a name for the rule into the dialog that opens.
The Plant Simulation conventions for the Name of an object do apply.
2. Click Table Condition. Enter your values into the table that opens.
• Click into the cell and select the level of the Opening Parenthesis. Plant Simulation evaluates
expressions in parenthesis from the inside to the outside, i.e., it evaluates the contents of the
innermost expression in parenthesis first and then proceeds toward the outside one by one.
• Enter the name of the Object whose attribute value you want to compare to another object.
• Enter the name of an Attribute of this object.
• Click into the cell and select an Operator that compares the attribute values: < (less than), >
(greater than), = (equal to), ~=(about equal), or /= (unequal to).
• Either enter the Value that the operator compares or the name of the Object whose attribute
value is compared to the object you entered above.
• Enter the name of an Attribute of this object.
• Click into the cell and select the level of the Closing Parenthesis.
• Click into the cell Boolean Operation and select an operator, AND or OR that connects the
boolean values that result from evaluating the logical expression you defined in a row of the query
table.
3. If you want to, you can also test a condition in a Method. For this, click Method Condition and enter
your source code into the Method that opens.
Create a Rule of Your Own
Plant Simulation Help 10-879
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

4. Click Table Action. Enter your values into the table that opens:
• Enter the name of the object whose attribute value you want to manipulate.
• Enter the name of an attribute of this object.
• Click into the cell and select an operator that manipulates the attribute value.
• Enter the value that the operator adds, subtracts or equates to the value of the attribute you
entered above.
5. If you want to, you can also program a Method that executes an action. To do so, click Method
Action and enter your source code into the Method that opens.
Back to Set Dynamic Parameters
Optimizing Models with Genetic Algorithms
Optimize Models with Genetic Algorithms
When your optimization task has a large number of different solution variants, we recommend to use
Genetic Algorithms.
Optimizing Models with Genetic Algorithms
10-880 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The GAWizard 
  integrates genetic algorithms into an existing simulation model. You can use it for
optimizations in which the evaluation is based on a simulation run as well as for optimizations in which the
evaluation is based on calculations in Methods.
Note
Your Plant Simulation license determines how you can use the GAWizard.
Genetic Algorithms are stochastic optimization procedures that in most cases only produce an
approximate solution. For the most common practical applications these approximate solutions do suffice.
Genetic algorithms manage several solution proposals during the optimization. According to evolution in
biology these solution proposals are called individuals. The individuals managed in a single optimization
step are combined into a generation. The quality of a solution proposal is evaluated through a numerical
value, the so-called fitness value.
Solutions generated by Genetic Algorithms are passed to the simulation model, which in turn will be
configured accordingly. Plant Simulation starts one or several simulation runs for each solution attempt. At
the end of a simulation run the simulation model is going to pass the resulting fitness value back to the
object GAWizard.
When your evaluation takes place via a model containing random components, such as failures of
machines, then you have to execute several simulation runs for each newly created individual. The
fitness value is called observation. The genetic algorithm uses the mean value of all observations of the
fitness values of an individual. Enter the number of observations per individual on the tab Define of the
GAWizard. For a deterministic simulation, you will use a single observation per individual.
When the genetic algorithm creates individuals defining the same parameterization, the GAWizard
recognizes this and uses the fitness values of the already evaluated individuals. The optimization run
thus does not waste additional time for multiple evaluations of an individual.
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Back to Run Simulation Experiments
Optimize Models with Genetic Algorithms
Plant Simulation Help 10-881
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Configure the GAWizard
In this step we configure the GAWizard.
Proceed as follows to add the GAWizard:
Procedure
1. Click the toolbar Tools and insert the GAWizard into your simulation model. Double-click the icon to
open the dialog of the GAWizard.
You can define the optimization problem with the GAWizard by using:
• Drag-and-Drop: Hold down Shift and drag the tables, the sequence of which you would like to
optimize onto the GAWizard and drop it there. When you would like to optimize an attribute
of an object, hold down Shift and drag this object onto the GAWizard, drop it and select the
attribute. Then, you can change the value range of the parameters to be optimized into the table
ProblemDefinition. To open this table, click the button Open next to Definition of the optimization
parameter on the tab Define.
Drag the object, whose attributes and methods you would like to use for evaluating the created
solutions, onto the GAWizard and drop them there. Then, select the attributes and methods of the
object. To weight these values differently, click Open next to Fitness calculation by table.
• If you use the built-in GA objects for describing your optimization problem, first define a
configuration method on the Tab Define of the GAWizard. Then hold down Shift and drag the
GA objects (GASelection and/or GASequence and/or GARangeAllocation and/or GASetAllocation)
onto the Wizard and click Apply. This registers all GA objects with the GAWizard. Then edit the
configuration method. The attribute individual designates the table of the individual that is to be
evaluated and that the genetic algorithm created. The first line of the source code shows how you
can access the chromosomes of the individual. With the configuration method you can transmit all
properties of the chromosomes to your simulation model. The result of the method is true when
a parametrization can be executed with the values of the individual. When the parametrization
fails, the respective individual will be assigned a bad fitness value, a so-called penalty value. The
penalty value for unrealizable individuals is the attribute penaltyValue of the GAWizard. To change
the value of this attribute, select the GAWizard in the Frame, into which you inserted it, and press
the F8 key.
Configure the GAWizard
10-882 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

2. Select the direction of the optimization: Minimum or Maximum. Enter the Size of generation and
the Number of generations on the tab Define.
3. You can determine the fitness value with the GAWizard by using:
• The table Fitness. Select the radio button Fitness calculation by table on the tab Define and click
Open. This opens the table Fitness. Enter the names of the values into the cells of the column
Target value. When the fitness value is determined by several target values, enter their weighting
into the cells of the column Weighting.
• You can let a method compute the fitness. To do so, select the respective radio button, insert a
Method, and click Apply. If you want to, the Wizard can prepare a Method for computing the
fitness. The return value is the fitness value and has to have a numeric data type, such as real or
time for example
4. Click Reset and Start on the tab Run to start the optimization.
You can stop the optimization run after the individuals of the active generation have been completely
evaluated and then modify the settings and the parameters of the object GAWizard. During the simulation
run, you can click Stop on the tab Run. As long as Plant Simulation is still evaluating the individuals of the
active generation the button shows Wait. When the button shows Start again you can click it to continue
the optimization run for creating new generations.
Configure the GAWizard
Plant Simulation Help 10-883
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

After Plant Simulation is finished with the optimization run, it shows a dialog with the time the
optimization run took. The wizard transfers the input values for the best solution it found into the
simulation model. You can now analyze how the optimized model behaves.
To start additional optimization runs, click Reset and Start on the tab Run or click the icon of the
GAWizard with the right mouse button and select Reset or Start and Stop on the context menu.
Note
The number of required simulation runs for an optimization can become quite large value.
For a stochastic simulation the individuals should be evaluated by several simulation runs. You can
enter the number of simulation runs for evaluating an individual on the tab Define > Observations per
individual. The number of individuals to be evaluated results from the Number of generations and
from the Size of generation. You can enter both values on the tab Define as well.
In the first generation Plant Simulation evaluates the number of generations which you entered as the
Size of generation. In each of the following generations it has to evaluate twice as many individuals.
When, for example, you use 3 observations for evaluating an individual and create 10 generations with a
frequently used Size of generation of 30, Plant Simulation has to execute 1710 simulation runs (3 * (30
+ 2 * 30 * 9)).
The number of simulation runs, which the GAWizard executes, results from this formula:
Number of simulation runs = observations per individual * (generation size + 2 * generation size *
(number of generations - 1))
The individuals created at a generation change of the Genetic Algorithm might already have been
created and evaluated in a previous generation. If the number of individuals to be generated is not
greater than 50,000 the GAWizard recognizes these individuals and applies the fitness values. Such
individuals have the same ID in the table Statistics of Fitness Values on the tab Evaluate. The attribute
lastGeneration4DuplicatesCheck of the GAWizard describes the last generation up to which the already
evaluated individuals will be checked. If you use the default value -1, the GAWizard runs this check for all
generations.
Analyze the Results of the Optimization
On the tab Run you can observe the optimization process. It shows the currently best fitness value at any
time. Click Show Evolution on the tab Evaluate to open a diagram displaying the best, the average and
the worst fitness values of each generation.
You can, for example, detect, if additional optimization runs with the active settings produce better results
or not.
Analyze the Results of the Optimization
10-884 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Click Show in the group HTML Report on the tab Evaluate to show the results and the course of the
optimization in a Report. When a very great number of individuals have been created, we recommend to
clear the check box Show detailed HTML report.
You can, for example, detect, if the genetic algorithm is searching for the best results on the level of almost
identical fitness values or if the fitness values of the offspring is still fluctuating quite a bit. For a stochastic
simulation the chart shows Min-Max intervals of the fitness values of an individual an. When the fitness
values fluctuate greatly, we recommend to check if an optimization of your model makes sense or not.
To analyze the statistics table of the fitness values, click the respective buttons. Column 7 of the statistics
table shows if individuals were created multiple times but evaluated once only.
Click Show next to Data of the report on the tab Evaluate to show the results and the history of the
optimization. When Plant Simulation created a large number of individuals, we recommend to not select
the check box Show detailed report.
Note
You can use the attribute DecimalSeparator of data type string to set the values "." (period) or ","
(comma) as the decimal separator. The results tables then use this decimal separator, compare Text File
Format > Decimal Separator.
